{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן בְּיָדֵנוּ זְבָחִים וְעֹלֹת</b> — Moses did not say this thing on condition of doing it, and he did not do so at all; rather, they are words of reinforcement. He means to say that the hand of HaShem will weigh heavily upon him and upon his people, to the point that he will even give sacrifices and burnt offerings and all that he has on behalf of his life. And in truth, when he said to them (Exodus 12:32) \"and you shall bless me as well,\" he would have given willingly all his livestock to atone for himself, but it never arose on Moses' mind to perform \"the sacrifice of the wicked is an abomination\" (Proverbs 21:27), for HaShem desired to crush him, not to atone for him, but only to punish him and to shake him and all his army in the sea. And our Rabbis said (Mekhilta, chapter 13; Tanḥuma, Bo 7) that \"as you have spoken\" (Exodus 12:32) refers to their statement <b>גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן בְּיָדֵנוּ זְבָחִים וְעֹלֹת</b>. Perhaps they meant to say that he hinted to them to give everything they had spoken of — not that they took from him at all — or sacrifices and burnt offerings for them in order that they might depart, not to offer them on his behalf. But even this is not correct:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:39.517531481Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}